As promised [here](https://github.com/ItsDoot/bevy_mod_check_filter/pull/1#issuecomment-1214363253) in #1, I've made a prototype implementation that uses lenses to allow checking different fields in one component. I recommend you look at `examples/lens-derive.rs` to get a full example, but the gist is this:

```rs
#[derive(Component, FieldCheckable)]
struct Enemy {
    state: EnemyState,
    condition: EnemyCondition,
}

#[derive(FieldCheckable)]
struct EnemyCondition {
    alive: EnemyAlive,
    health: u32,
}

#[derive(FieldCheckable)]
struct EnemyAlive {
    alive: bool,
}

fn running_enemies(_entities: Query<&Enemy, Check<lens!(Enemy::state), IsRunning>>) {
    // ...
}

fn alive_enemies(
    _entities: Query<
        &Enemy,
        Check<lens!(Enemy::condition, EnemyCondition::alive, EnemyAlive::alive), Is<true>>,
    >,
) {
    // ...
}
```

The lenses allow you to access different fields, and they're composable, so you can access nested structs. I think it's pretty neat :)

It's still very rough, but I'm opening the PR so you can take a look and say if this is something you'd be interested in, and if you are, I'll polish it a bit more. Let me know if you want any names changed. It builds on #1, but in case that PR is rejected and this isn't, it could be reworked to remove the other stuff.

To make it work I added the `Checkable` trait, which substitutes `Component` as the trait bound on `T` inside `Check`. This trait has two associated types: `Component` and `Checked`. These are not good names, I'm still working on replacements '^^. `Component` is the actual component, and `Checked` is the type that is fed into the `test` method of `Predicate`. I shifted the stuff in `src/impls.rs` to refer to `T::Component` instead of `T`. 

Last thing: I haven't actually tested that it filters stuff correctly, I'll set up some tests for that later.

**Please describe the changes this PR makes and why it should be merged:**

`interface.options.getRole` return type is `Role | APIRole | Null`.  `APIRole` isn't include in `RoleResolvable` (which is defined as `Role |  Snowflake`) so when trying to implement a function like this:
```ts
async execute(interaction: CommandInteraction) {
        await interaction.deferReply({ ephemeral: true });

        const mutedRole = interaction.options.getRole("muted")!;

        const botHighestRole = interaction.guild!.me!.roles.highest;
        const canBotManageRole =
                botHighestRole.comparePositionTo(mutedRole) > 0;
        // ...
}
```

you get this type error in typescript:

```
Argument of type 'Role | APIRole' is not assignable to parameter of type 'RoleResolvable'.
  Type 'APIRole' is not assignable to type 'RoleResolvable'.
    Type 'APIRole' is missing the following properties from type 'Role': createdAt, createdTimestamp, deleted, editable, and 21 more.
```

The missing properties of `APIRole` aren't needed to resolve the role, what you only need is the id/Snowflake of the role which is present. There shouldn't be an error here so we should accept `APIRole` as a valid `RoleResolvable` type.



**Status and versioning classification:**

- Code changes have been tested against the Discord API, or there are no code changes
- I know how to update typings and have done so, or typings don't need updating
- This PR **only** includes non-code changes, like changes to documentation, README, etc.

